Jove <invalid@invalid.invalid> wrote:

>   FYI: Using 'diff' will give results that can be (more or less)
> automatically added using 'patch'.  The actual 'diff' command
> line should be included in the 'patch' file, as 'patch' is rather
> good about understand 'diff' output.  An example line for diff'ing
> an individual file would be:

> diff -NurbBX ignore.txt Pristine/src/mapglyph.c Prompt_Fix/src/mapglyph.c

You shouldn't use -bB, and you should use -p. If you had to do whitespace
cleanup, it was because otherwise the files would have the wrong indention,
and -p gives you the name of the changed fuction (nice to have if you
review a patch).

Additionally, you should generate patches that can be applied with -p1,
since AFAIK this is the usural convention.

For more infos, please google for 'submittingpatches'. This guide is made
for the linux kernel, but IMO it's generally usefull. (Ignore the
signed-off-by stuff and the description delimiter ('---'), it's git-specific)
